# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# configuration.properties.example - template for the runtime configuration
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Usage
#   Copy this file to "configuration.properties" (drop the .example suffix)
#   and fill in a value for each key. No values are supplied here: the correct
#   ones depend on the instance being tested and are not part of this project.
#
# Where the file must live
#   The reader opens the bare relative filename "configuration.properties", so
#   it resolves against the process working directory - the repository root
#   when the test and report commands are run from there.
#
# Never commit the real file
#   "configuration.properties" is deliberately git-ignored. It holds sign-in
#   credentials for the system under test; keep it out of version control.
#
# Nothing is required at startup
#   A missing file is logged once and the run continues; a missing key is
#   reported at the point of use rather than at startup. That tolerance is
#   intended behaviour, carried over unchanged, so unit tests, the report
#   viewer, the artifact writers and dry runs all work without this file.
#   Only scenarios that drive a real browser need it filled in.
#
# Read once per worker process
#   The file is read and cached on first access in each worker process and is
#   never re-read. A change made while a run is in progress therefore has no
#   effect on a reader that has already initialised.
#
# Format
#   java.util.Properties, not INI. There are no section headers; a line
#   beginning with "#" (or "!") is a comment; "=" separates a key from its
#   value. Keys are case-sensitive and are read by these exact names.
#
# Keys
#   Six keys, and only six. Nothing reads any other name, so adding a key here
#   has no effect - it would simply be ignored.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Selects the browser the driver constructs. Exactly two values are
# recognised: chrome or firefox. There is no default and no fallback, so any
# other value - or none at all - leaves the driver unset and the scenario
# fails when it first tries to use it.
browser=

# Address of the sign-in page. Used by the login steps, by the shared
# precondition step that other features' backgrounds invoke, and by the
# opening step of the Employee flow.
web.table.url=

# Address of the Employee module. Read only by the Employee flow, which
# navigates here three times. This is a separate key from web.table.url
# above; the two point at different pages and are not interchangeable.
url=

# Sign-in name used by the shared precondition step that other features'
# backgrounds invoke.
username=

# Password for that same shared precondition step. Treat it as a secret: it
# belongs only in the git-ignored copy of this file.
password=

# Page title the Employee flow waits for and asserts after navigating to the
# Employee module. Must match that page's title exactly, or the wait expires
# and the scenario fails.
EmplTitle=
